PROPOSED — NOT APPLIED. T9-W7 §10 pass 4 · CTRL-TAPE.

W2's §2.6 row (`e2e/viewport-law.spec.ts`, `STICKY_CENSUS`) is RED on this tree in BOTH engines
at both its cells, and the red is LATENCY, not the pose. `STICKY_CENSUS` writes `card.scrollTop`
and reads `getComputedStyle(tag).position` in the SAME synchronous block; `data-released` — the
release-at-half pose this family ships — is published by an `IntersectionObserver`, which cannot
have run in that task. The row therefore reads the pose the tape held at `scrollTop 0`.

MEASURED, both engines, both cells (`instruments/p4-sticky.mjs`, `logs/p4-sticky.log`):

  cell                 tag        SYNC (the row's own read)   SETTLED (+400 ms)
  rail 1440×900        new game   sticky · visFrac 1          sticky · visFrac 1
  rail 1440×900        pencils    static · visFrac 0          sticky · visFrac 1   wellInView 0.856
  drawer 375×667       new game   sticky · visFrac 1          sticky · visFrac 1
  drawer 375×667       pencils    static · visFrac 0          sticky · visFrac 1   wellInView 0.811

WHY THE CONTROL IS GREEN AND THIS TREE IS NOT. At `74a2b5d9` the card's scroll range is 504 px
(rail) / 248 px (drawer) and `pencils` needs 599.2 / 361.7, so the row reports it `unreachable`
and never reads a second tape at all. This design's card is taller — range 717 / 433 — so the
second compartment becomes reachable and the row reads it for the first time. The design did not
break the row; it made the row's second subject exist.

THE ROW IS W2's AND THE CHAIR'S TO MOVE (LAWS §The record: an instrument whose subject a design
moved is a PROPOSED diff and the row is reported MOVED). It is NOT re-worded to pass: the
assertion, the thresholds and the 40 px offset are untouched. The only change is that the census
lets the pose it asserts be published before it reads it, which is the same settle the dock sheet
